00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.135
So thank you all for coming,
and I hope it's a good session.

00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:38.760
So the first thing
I want to talk about

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.450
is this topic of big metadata.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.890
Everybody throws around
the term big data,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:43.960
which is cool in itself--

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.567
BigQuery excels at processing
huge volumes of data.

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.400
But the thing that I
want to talk about here

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.560
is actually the data
around that data,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.670
or as I like to call it,
big metadata, in this case.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.080
So for a bit of an overview,
BigQuery's architecture

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.193
looks something like this.

00:00:58.193 --> 00:00:59.610
There's a lot of
pieces I omitted,

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.440
but you can generally
think of the system

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.860
as being roughly this shape.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.218
So we have things here
like, you can get data

00:01:05.218 --> 00:01:06.510
in through streaming ingestion.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.800
There's bulk loading.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.420
We have this data
transfer service

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.270
where you can get data in that
I didn't even picture here.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.890
We have interfaces like the UI.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.992
We have client
libraries, et cetera.

00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:17.700
Some of the cool pieces
behind the scenes

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.552
are things like our high
available compute cluster.

00:01:20.552 --> 00:01:22.260
This is where we
actually run the queries

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.925
that you give to us.

00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:25.050
We have our storage system.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.383
And then what ties this all
together is this really fast

00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:29.940
network that Google built.
It's hard to imagine

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.680
building this
system anywhere else

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.888
just because we have such
a great infrastructure that

00:01:33.888 --> 00:01:34.721
makes this possible.

00:01:34.721 --> 00:01:36.471
So the thing that I
want to focus on today

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:38.130
is the data that
we actually manage

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.120
behind the scenes for you
and what goes into that.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.560
So I want to start by talking
about what makes a table.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.560
So we've got a variety
of attributes of a table.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.930
So there's things like
the table ID itself.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.310
This is like the name
of it, so to speak.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.918
We have this schema
of the table,

00:01:52.918 --> 00:01:54.960
various attributes like
whether it's partitioned,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.377
what partition in column there
is, whether it's clustered,

00:01:57.377 --> 00:01:58.928
that sort of thing.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:00.970
We have the amount of data
that is being stored--

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:02.762
so the number of bytes,
the number of rows,

00:02:02.762 --> 00:02:03.948
that kind of thing.

00:02:03.948 --> 00:02:05.490
We have the description
of the table.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.039
You can edit this
yourself, for example.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.160
And then we have the
actual data itself.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.180
So that's great, but what
really makes a table?

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.580
What is the actual
representation

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.250
behind the scenes of
tables in BigQuery?

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.272
So we use this format
that we call capacitor.

00:02:22.272 --> 00:02:24.230
There's a great blog post
about this, actually.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.510
I don't have enough time to
go into the details of this,

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.968
but capacitor is
a format that lets

00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:31.710
us really efficiently represent
nested and repeated data.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.240
And this is what
we use in BigQuery.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.920
We developed this
format ourself.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.510
This is sort of a
secret sauce, so

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.020
to speak, of BigQuery that
makes queries really fast.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.470
So we store this data
for you in this format.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.780
And when we store these
capacitor files, all of these

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:46.980
are encrypted at rest.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.483
They're durable, meaning that
if one copy of a file on a disk

00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:51.900
ends up getting
deleted or there's

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.025
a problem with the hardware
and that kind of thing,

00:02:54.025 --> 00:02:55.230
we're not going to lose it.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:56.530
It's also highly available.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.830
So if part of a data
center is not operational,

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.200
we're able to still
serve you that data.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.670
And there's no chance of
it being lost, essentially.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.530
So the thing that
we do is that we

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.890
have this concept that
builds on these files called

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.060
storage sets.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.115
So storage sets are a grouping
of these capacitor files.

00:03:16.115 --> 00:03:18.240
And a storage set is a
combination of a few things.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.260
So it's a path to the files
in our distributed storage.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.600
It has a partition key
for partition tables.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.370
It has a timestamp
of when we actually

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.700
committed this data, when
we wrote it to our metadata.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.760
It has a UUID--

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.102
so some unique identifier
for that storage set.

00:03:34.102 --> 00:03:35.560
And then it has a
variety of stats.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.110
So this includes things like
column sizes, for example.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.680
So when you run a
query against a table,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.718
we fetch these column
sizes from these storage

00:03:42.718 --> 00:03:44.760
sets to figure out how
much data will actually be

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.597
scanned as part of that query.

00:03:46.597 --> 00:03:47.430
Then it has a state.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.763
So it can be in a pending state.

00:03:48.763 --> 00:03:49.820
It can be committed.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.320
Or it can be in a garbage state,
meaning that it was deleted

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.073
or is no longer applicable.

00:03:54.073 --> 00:03:55.740
So to give you a sense
of scale of this,

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.282
we have on the order of billions
of storage sets in BigQuery.

00:03:58.282 --> 00:03:59.940
I just ran a query last night.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.770
This is a current
snapshot of the system.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.840
So this tends to
change over time.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.250
But currently, it's on
the order of billions.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.160
So that's a lot of metadata.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.190
So why do we do this?

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.760
Why do we have these
storage sets in BigQuery?

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.550
Well, the reason that we do this
is, it's the unit of atomicity.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:17.959
And what I mean by that
is that it gives us

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:18.834
properties like this.

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:22.970
So when I select from a table
at a particular timestamp, what

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.343
it means is that I
ignore any stored

00:04:25.343 --> 00:04:27.260
sets that are in a pending
or a garbage state.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.940
I only read the ones that were
committed as of that timestamp

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.970
at which I'm reading the data.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.580
If they weren't
committed at that point,

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.040
if they were committed
later, then I ignore them.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.250
So when they perform operations
like loading data or DML

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.950
queries, all that sort of thing,
all these operations either

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.072
succeed or they don't.

00:04:44.072 --> 00:04:45.530
And when you run
a query, you never

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.720
see partially-written data.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.780
So the property that we provide
is known as snapshot isolation.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.040
Basically, you never
have to worry that when

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.560
you run a query that you'll see
the partial state of the table.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.840
Because we have this unit
of atomicity called storage

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.000
sets, when we persist
that data, either you're

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.110
seeing all of it or none.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.520
So what creates storage sets?

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.140
So when you load
it into the system,

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.197
we create a storage
set per partition,

00:05:09.197 --> 00:05:10.780
or when you stream
data in, what we do

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.030
is we actually buffer that
data that you're sending us

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.950
and then periodically flush this
data into our managed storage.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.252
When you run a create
table select statement,

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:22.210
we're actually taking
the results of that query

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.300
and writing it to storage
sets behind the scenes.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.220
Or when you run DML statements--

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.520
update, insert, et cetera, we're
creating storage sets, as well,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.947
as part of those operations.

00:05:32.947 --> 00:05:34.780
So let's look at how
this works in practice.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.910
Imagine I had this query where
I'm selecting from a table.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:38.993
So I'm selecting these
couple of columns, x and y,

00:05:38.993 --> 00:05:40.032
from this table here.

00:05:40.032 --> 00:05:40.740
So what do we do?

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.570
First, what we do is,
we list the storage

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.640
sets in our Spanner metadata.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:45.710
We use Spanner.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:46.630
Spanner is awesome.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.630
This is how we keep track
of all of our metadata

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:49.647
behind the scenes.

00:05:49.647 --> 00:05:51.480
So we list the storage
sets for these tables

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.020
from our Spanner metadata.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.190
And then we send a request
to our query engine

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.960
that describes the mapping of
these tables to these storage

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.420
sets that we're going to
read as part of the query.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.760
As a brief architecture, there's
a lot of pieces missing here,

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.510
but you can roughly
think of it like this.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.050
So as a client,
you send a request

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.628
with a query to BigQuery.

00:06:10.628 --> 00:06:11.920
There's a couple of steps here.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.430
We have this router
behind the scenes that

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:14.960
figures out where
to actually send

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.560
the query when we receive it.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:17.930
It goes to this job server.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:19.910
We have these Spanner
tables for job metadata,

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.023
table metadata, et cetera.

00:06:21.023 --> 00:06:22.940
And what we're doing is,
we're looking up data

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.480
for the tables
that you reference.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.022
And then we use that as part of
execution in our query engine

00:06:27.022 --> 00:06:28.728
itself.

00:06:28.728 --> 00:06:31.270
And you can see down here, so
we have our distributed storage

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.330
system with capacitor files
that are actually on disk.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.550
And when we go to read a
table, what we're doing is,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:37.925
we're reading the
capacitor files

00:06:37.925 --> 00:06:39.640
relevant to that
particular table that's

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:40.557
being referenced here.

00:06:43.395 --> 00:06:45.520
So how does this work for
partition tables, though?

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:47.680
So for partition
tables, we actually

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.320
have one or more storage
sets for each day

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:51.860
for which you have data.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.950
So in this example, I
have data for the 7th,

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:57.100
the 8th, the 10th, and so on.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:58.990
And so I can have one
or more storage sets

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:00.640
for each of those days.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.860
Storage sets can
span partitions.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.780
So what happens when
I run a query is

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.910
that when I select
from a partition table,

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.610
I match any of these storage
sets for the days that

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:12.570
are referenced.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.240
So in this example, I'm
selecting data for the 10th.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.150
So what I do is
that, as BigQuery,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.650
when I'm fetching
the storage that's

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.550
relevant to the query at hand,
I filter out the ones outside

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.623
of the 10th here.

00:07:24.623 --> 00:07:26.040
So because I had
this filter where

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:27.810
I restricted the data I
was reading to the 10th,

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.260
it means I could ignore any
storage sets outside of that.

00:07:33.525 --> 00:07:35.150
So you may be familiar
with the concept

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.380
of clustering in BigQuery.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.338
What clustering lets you
do is actually lay out

00:07:38.338 --> 00:07:41.030
the data in your tables
according to some clustering

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:42.103
keys.

00:07:42.103 --> 00:07:43.520
And when you do
this, what happens

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.530
is that we're
actually laying out

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.560
the files based on
the clustering keys

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.100
that you provided us.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:51.680
So these storage set files are
sorted by the cluster keys,

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.510
essentially.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:54.930
So what happens in this case?

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.930
So when you select
from a table that's

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.142
both partitioned and
clustered and you

00:07:59.142 --> 00:08:00.600
have a query that
looks like this--

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.950
so I'm restricting the data that
I'm reading to the 10th, here.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.350
And then I'm restricting
further that the price is

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.540
between these two
different values.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.540
So if my table was clustered
by price, what I would do

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.870
is that first, I
would apply that date

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:15.330
filter that you gave me.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.362
So I would restrict the
data being read to the 10th.

00:08:18.362 --> 00:08:19.820
And then further,
what I can do is,

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.430
because you gave me this
filter on this price column,

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.497
I can filter out more of
the data behind the scenes.

00:08:25.497 --> 00:08:27.080
And then this is
passed along in terms

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.720
of reduced query time,
and also a lower query

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:30.860
cost for the query itself.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:38.059
So that was on
selecting from a table.

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:40.309
But what happens when I
actually update a table?

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:43.090
So when I go and update a
partition table, what we do is,

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.220
we list the storage
sets that match whatever

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.150
filter you gave us, if any.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:50.680
So in this case, I have this
filter on the 8th or the 10th.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.180
And then what I do
is, I run a query

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.580
and I actually rewrite
the effective storage sets

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.090
for this query.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:58.220
So what this looks like
is that, in this case,

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.220
I've restricted the
data being modified

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.810
to these particular
storage sets here

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.203
based on that filter
that you gave me.

00:09:04.203 --> 00:09:05.620
And then what I
end up doing is, I

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:09.010
decide that, based on
running this query,

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:11.360
I end up modifying
these storage sets.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.697
And then I replace
these, essentially,

00:09:13.697 --> 00:09:16.030
and mark the old ones as being
in, now, a garbage state.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.620
And now I have this
new data that I've

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.930
committed for this table.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:21.410
And again, all this is atomic.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.970
So if somebody else is running
a query at the same time,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.450
they're not going to see
the partially-modified state

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:31.340
of the storage while
it's in transit.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.785
So we also allow simultaneous
updates on petition tables.

00:09:33.785 --> 00:09:35.410
I can actually have
two updates running

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.660
at the same time that
modify different dates

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:38.673
within this table.

00:09:38.673 --> 00:09:40.090
So in this example,
I have a query

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:42.937
that's modifying both
the 9th and 10th here--

00:09:42.937 --> 00:09:44.270
or, sorry, the 8th and the 10th.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.180
And then I have another one
that's modifying the 7th.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:47.763
And these are OK as
long as they don't

00:09:47.763 --> 00:09:48.960
have conflicts between them.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.710
So I have one query
that goes and rewrites

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.460
the storage sets for
the 8th and the 10th,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.520
and then one that's
modifying the 7th.

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.475
And there's no conflict,
because the queries

00:09:56.475 --> 00:09:57.850
are reading and
modifying storage

00:09:57.850 --> 00:09:59.725
sets in different
partitions as part of this.

00:10:02.726 --> 00:10:04.980
So we also provide this
really cool feature,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.300
I think, called Time Travel.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.480
So the notion of Time
Travel is that because we

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.228
have these storage sets that
we're keeping for your tables,

00:10:12.228 --> 00:10:13.770
we let you travel
back in time to see

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.660
the historical
version of a table

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.280
as it was up to seven
days in the past.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.682
So we have these storage
sets that we're keeping.

00:10:20.682 --> 00:10:22.140
When you go and
run a query and add

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.960
data, for example, or committing
new storage sets, what

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.270
you can do is that
when you run this query

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.340
with this clause that
says for system time as

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.277
of some timestamp,
we can go and fetch

00:10:31.277 --> 00:10:33.360
the storage sets that were
in a committed state as

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:34.840
of that timestamp.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.630
And that means that
you can read the data

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.850
as it was prior to a load job
or prior to some DML query,

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:40.497
for example.

00:10:40.497 --> 00:10:42.330
So if you run a query
and you realize, oops,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.040
I deleted all the
rows in my table,

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.560
I'd really like to get
that data back, you

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.960
can go and use this
feature called Time Travel

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.420
and use this clause to actually
read that data as it was

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.935
before you made that operation.

00:10:54.935 --> 00:10:56.060
So where can this go wrong?

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.143
How can these storage sites
end up in a bad state?

00:10:58.143 --> 00:10:59.790
Or are things just always happy?

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:03.565
So storage sets can end up
in a bad state for a couple

00:11:03.565 --> 00:11:04.440
of different reasons.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.710
So you can end up with
a really large number

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:08.390
of very small
storage sets-- end up

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.600
having, essentially, way more
metadata than you do data

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.890
if you do things like
microbatch your load job.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.263
So if you load really
small files, like,

00:11:16.263 --> 00:11:17.930
let's say, one every
minute, and it only

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:19.850
has a few rows at a
time, what ends up

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.370
happening is that we have,
essentially, more metadata

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:25.470
than we do data for that table.

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:27.835
So you can also do things
like stream at really low QPS.

00:11:27.835 --> 00:11:29.960
So if you're streaming,
like, one row every minute,

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.257
what we end up doing is,
when we flush that data,

00:11:32.257 --> 00:11:34.340
we're writing very small
storage sets periodically

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.800
to that table.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:40.170
Or if you perform very small
DML queries-- at least for now.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:41.990
So this is sort of
a snapshot in time.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.060
So the way that
DML works currently

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.700
is that we're committing a
storage set for each of the DML

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.365
queries that you perform.

00:11:48.365 --> 00:11:50.240
I say this is a snapshot
in time because this

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.580
is subject to change
in the future.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:55.148
So what's the actual
symptom of this?

00:11:55.148 --> 00:11:57.190
Well, for one thing,
queries can take a long time

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.695
to fetch metadata for executing.

00:11:58.695 --> 00:12:00.570
I mentioned that we do
that read from Spanner

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.220
of all those storage sets.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.800
So if you have, let's
say, one per minute

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.720
that you are creating
as part of these loads,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:08.470
we can end up with
more than 1,000 storage

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:09.720
sets over the course of a day.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.137
And so we have to
go and fetch all

00:12:11.137 --> 00:12:13.530
of those, which includes
things like the column sizes

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:14.047
and so on.

00:12:14.047 --> 00:12:15.630
That's a lot to
actually fit in memory

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.035
and pass around the system.

00:12:17.035 --> 00:12:18.660
And so your queries
can actually end up

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:19.770
taking a bit longer
than you would

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.570
expect even though
it may be processing

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:23.990
a small amount of data.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.420
Or because we use the
number of files and storage

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:29.510
sets, for example, as
a hint for parallelism.

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:31.190
We can end up using
more workers to try

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:35.680
to process your data than
we would normally do.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.360
So what can you do instead?

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:38.777
Well, as you may
have guessed, you

00:12:38.777 --> 00:12:40.690
can perform less frequent,
larger load jobs.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:44.360
So we provide the ability
to specify a glob pattern.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:46.320
And you can load a
bunch of data at a time

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.120
by matching a pattern that's
on cloud storage, for example.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.010
Or you can stream
at a higher QPS.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.000
Basically, use the
right API for the job.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.510
So if you have a lot of data
that's flowing continuously

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.552
over time, you probably
want to use the streaming

00:12:58.552 --> 00:13:00.565
API rather than the load API.

00:13:00.565 --> 00:13:01.940
On the flipside,
if you have data

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.060
that's writing very
infrequently in larger batches,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.140
use the load API.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:10.698
Or, execute larger DML queries.

00:13:10.698 --> 00:13:12.240
So if you have a
lot of rows that you

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.910
want to modify, instead
of running 10 DML queries,

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.130
for example, just run one
that modifies all of them

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:19.833
in one pass.

00:13:19.833 --> 00:13:22.000
But even if you don't,
there's a silver lining here.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.140
So BigQuery manages
your data for you.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:27.080
And what I mean by this is that
even if you have these storage

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.070
sets in bad state, BigQuery
has a background process that

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:31.270
goes and fixes that for you.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:34.060
So you don't need to worry
about this, to some extent.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.910
So even if you don't follow
these best practices right,

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:38.380
we have this
optimization process

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.780
running behind the scenes that
fixes up your data for you

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:43.322
and gets it into a better state
so that your queries are then

00:13:43.322 --> 00:13:47.320
faster as a result.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:50.840
So let's transition a bit to
the topic of querying metadata.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.500
So we recently released
something cool called

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:55.160
INFORMATION_SCHEMA views.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.050
So if you're familiar
with the SQL standard--

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.248
I've read it probably
more than I would like--

00:13:59.248 --> 00:14:00.790
there's this chapter
called Schemata.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:03.190
So schemata describes these
INFORMATION_SCHEMA views.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:07.120
They give you a structured
way of referencing metadata

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:08.440
in the system.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:11.440
So far in BigQuery, this
means that we include

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.240
things like schemata view.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.500
This gives you
data set, metadata.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.320
We have table-related views.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.740
There's a view about
views themselves.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:22.992
We have columns information.

00:14:22.992 --> 00:14:24.700
And we have a
BigQuery-specific extension

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.710
called COLUMN_FIELD_PATHS
that gives you

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:30.760
an overview of the nested
data that you have.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:33.940
And we're going to keep
adding more over time.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.422
So as a very quick
overview of these, so

00:14:36.422 --> 00:14:37.630
we have these schemata views.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:39.710
And this gives you
data set metadata.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.890
So in this example,
I'm running a query

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:43.900
that gives me the
locations of all the data

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:47.180
sets in this BigQuery public
data project broken down

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:47.680
by region.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.200
So I can see that I have four
data sets in the EU region,

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:54.060
and the rest appear to
be in the US region.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:55.220
I have table metadata.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:57.750
So I'm able to run this query
over the San Francisco data

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.980
set, for example, and
see these tables that

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:04.940
have the prefix
"bike," in this case.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.130
I have a views metadata table.

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.520
So this gives me information
about the actual definition

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:10.423
of these views.

00:15:10.423 --> 00:15:13.090
So I can go run a query and pull
the definition of all the views

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:14.298
that I've stored in BigQuery.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:17.910
There is the columns view.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:20.610
So this gives me information
about the column names

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.110
and their types,
which ones are used

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.360
for partitioning, which ones
are used for clustering--

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:25.300
that sort of thing.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.633
And I can go and run a query
to figure out, essentially,

00:15:27.633 --> 00:15:31.675
any information that I
want about these columns.

00:15:31.675 --> 00:15:33.550
We also have this
BigQuery-specific extension

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.080
called COLUMN_FIELD_PATHS.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:38.110
So this lets you actually
analyze the paths

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:39.110
to the data themselves.

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:41.230
So you can have strucs.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:41.740
For example.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.157
In BigQuery, you
can have arrays--

00:15:43.157 --> 00:15:44.030
that kind of thing.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:47.340
This basically gives you a
way of understanding the paths

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.130
to data that you can use.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:52.060
So in this example, I have
this inputs column and outputs

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:52.560
column.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.018
And both of these have fields
underneath them called value.

00:15:55.018 --> 00:15:57.018
And so I'm able to figure
out that both of those

00:15:57.018 --> 00:15:58.918
have numeric type and
I'm able to analyze

00:15:58.918 --> 00:16:00.460
that at that
[INAUDIBLE] field level.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:06.630
So I have a short
example on this.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.990
So I'm going to tie all these
concepts together in a query,

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:10.490
actually.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.657
If we could switch to the
demo, that would be great.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:18.020
So in this demo,
what I'm doing is,

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.600
I'm actually rebuilding
the create table statements

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.830
that I can use for my tables.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.550
So I have a variety
of attributes here.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.950
So one is, I have this function
for making the partition

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:31.070
by expression for my tables.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:32.920
I have this clause
that I'm generating.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:36.830
So here's the actual
partition by text itself.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:40.130
Cluster by-clause for
any cluster tables.

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.320
I build the column
[INAUDIBLE] itself.

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:44.510
Any options associated
with the table.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.650
And then I take these
various components

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.500
and I combine them
all together in order

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:51.990
to generate this create or
replace table statement.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.130
So what you can see here is that
I've generated these results.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:57.545
Let me zoom out a bit,
actually, so you can see better.

00:16:57.545 --> 00:16:59.420
So I've generated all
these create or replace

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.660
tables statements that recreate
the definitions of my tables

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.050
from these columns.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.720
And I have this partition by
attribute, options list, et

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:08.950
cetera.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:11.272
So that's pretty cool.

00:17:11.272 --> 00:17:13.730
You can also find this example
in our public documentation.

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:14.170
We added this.

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:15.712
If you search for
information schema,

00:17:15.712 --> 00:17:17.970
we show you how to
do this yourself.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:20.270
Can we switch back to
the slide deck, please?

00:17:23.618 --> 00:17:25.160
Something else I'd
like to talk about

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:30.630
is that we're announcing a new
feature around encrypting PII.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.750
So when you manage
PII in BigQuery,

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.628
there's a couple of things
that I want to mention first.

00:17:36.628 --> 00:17:38.170
So keep in mind that
data in BigQuery

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.317
is always encrypted at rest.

00:17:40.317 --> 00:17:42.400
Whenever we store your
data, those capacitor files

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:45.940
that are on disk are kept
in an encrypted format.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:49.600
Our API endpoints use HTTPS,
so clients encrypt requests,

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:50.110
as well.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:56.033
You can also use CMEK to encrypt
specific tables with a key,

00:17:56.033 --> 00:17:58.200
essentially that you manage
yourself if you want to.

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:01.380
And we provide an API called
Data Loss Prevention that

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.630
can help you to
classify and redact PII.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.880
These are just some things
to keep in mind, basically,

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.620
as I'm talking about this.

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:11.540
So here's the problem
statement, though.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.980
Tracking PII in your data
across many tables and data sets

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:14.480
is hard.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.147
You don't necessarily
know which columns

00:18:16.147 --> 00:18:19.980
have PII or any of the tables,
necessarily, that have PII.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:22.010
It's kind of hard to
track them all down.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.560
When you want to remove data
for a specific user, what

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.260
can be hard is to run
updates and deletes

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:28.947
across all the tables.

00:18:28.947 --> 00:18:30.530
It's hard to figure
out, first of all,

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:32.630
what you need to actually
run those queries over.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.713
And then it can also be
expensive to go and modify

00:18:34.713 --> 00:18:36.935
all those, too.

00:18:36.935 --> 00:18:39.310
So what I'm announcing today
is that encryption functions

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.450
in BigQuery are now
generally available.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.100
So this is a pretty
powerful feature,

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:44.440
but it's also very low-level.

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:46.940
And I want to talk a bit about
how you can actually leverage

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:48.322
this in your applications.

00:18:48.322 --> 00:18:50.530
These are functions for
creating key sets, performing

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:53.848
encryption, and decryption.

00:18:53.848 --> 00:18:57.340
And the core use case for this
is crypto-deletion of PII.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:59.248
You can keep any PII
in an encrypted format,

00:18:59.248 --> 00:19:01.540
And then you can perform
what's called crypto-deletion.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:05.780
And I'm going to show
you a bit how that works.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.000
So how does
crypto-deletion help?

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.000
So what you can do is, you can
encrypt each of your own user's

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:14.980
data with a different key.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:17.660
And then when you want to delete
their data, instead of tracking

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.660
it down across all the
tables that you may have,

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:21.690
what you can do is you
just throw away that key.

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:22.690
You just delete the key.

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:27.013
And now the data is
no longer recoverable.

00:19:27.013 --> 00:19:28.430
So to set this up,
what you can do

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:30.320
is that-- imagine that you
have this customer's table,

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:30.990
for example.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:33.480
And you want to generate a
key for each of your users.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.480
So you can go and add a
new column to the table.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.147
And then you can run
an update statement

00:19:37.147 --> 00:19:39.680
to actually populate the key
sets for each of the customers

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:40.457
that you have.

00:19:40.457 --> 00:19:42.290
So here, I'm using this
new key set function

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:46.998
and I'm generating a new key
set for each of my customers.

00:19:46.998 --> 00:19:48.540
Now what I can do
is that when I want

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:50.472
to encrypt data
using these key sets,

00:19:50.472 --> 00:19:51.930
I can run a query
like this where I

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.340
use this AEAD.ENCRYPT function.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:56.640
And what I do is, I
generate cipher texts

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:00.090
based on that input data
that I have, using the key

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:01.840
set for each of my customers.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.968
So now, each customer's data is
encrypted using their own key.

00:20:04.968 --> 00:20:07.260
I can give somebody access
to this customer data table,

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:08.130
for example.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.050
All this data is kept
in encrypted form.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:11.940
And so even if somebody
had access to this,

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.655
without the key sets,
it's not readable.

00:20:13.655 --> 00:20:15.780
There's nothing you could
do with that information,

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.260
because it's stored
in encrypted form.

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:21.310
I can also decrypt
data using key sets.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.952
So I can use this DECRYPT_STING
function, for example,

00:20:23.952 --> 00:20:26.410
and I can go ahead and recover
that data from that customer

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.570
data table using the
key sets that I have

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:29.770
stored in my customer table.

00:20:34.725 --> 00:20:37.100
And then, as I mentioned, so
when I want to crypto-delete

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:37.980
data--

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.730
so when I have a number
of inactive customers,

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:43.380
somebody asks for me to
delete their data, et cetera,

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:46.850
what I can do is, I can go
ahead and delete those key sets.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.692
So once those key
sets are gone, I

00:20:48.692 --> 00:20:50.900
don't have a way of getting
that encrypted data back.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.942
I have to use those
decryption functions in order

00:20:52.942 --> 00:20:54.360
to decrypt that data.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.060
And so once those key sets
are no longer available to me,

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.435
I can't recover
it at that point.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.910
So what's cool about this is
that you can perform encryption

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:07.170
outside of BigQuery, too.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:09.990
We have interoperability thanks
to the library that we use.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:12.540
So we use this open-source
library called Tink.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.790
There's a saying in the
industry that you should never

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:15.720
roll your own crypto.

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:17.678
We didn't try to make
that mistake in BigQuery.

00:21:17.678 --> 00:21:19.300
We use an open-source library.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:21.280
It's developed by security
engineers at Google,

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.370
so we have pretty high
confidence in this.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.230
So we use this
library called Tink.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:27.458
And what this lets you do
is that you can not only

00:21:27.458 --> 00:21:29.250
use these functions
within BigQuery itself,

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:31.333
but you can also use them
in whatever applications

00:21:31.333 --> 00:21:33.510
you build outside
of BigQuery, too.

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.990
So you can create key sets,
perform encryption, decryption,

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:39.930
et cetera either within your
own code or within BigQuery

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:42.280
directly.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:44.370
So imagine you have
a scenario where

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:46.710
you can encrypt using
Dataflow, for example,

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:48.060
and then decrypt using BigQuery.

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:49.500
So any data stored
in your tables

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:51.560
is stored in encrypted form.

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:53.100
But you encrypted
it using Dataflow.

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:54.558
And you can do that
because Tink is

00:21:54.558 --> 00:21:56.083
available as an
open-source library.

00:21:56.083 --> 00:21:58.500
You can use it from Dataflow
or whatever other application

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.250
that you're building.

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:03.170
Alternatively, maybe
you store your key sets

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:05.780
on cloud storage and your
encrypted data in BigQuery.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:08.280
So maybe you don't want to keep
the keys in BigQuery itself,

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:08.970
for example.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:11.030
You want to keep them on
files on cloud storage.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:12.530
What you can do in
that case is, you

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:15.350
can define an external table
over your cloud storage

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:16.255
key sets.

00:22:16.255 --> 00:22:17.630
And then you can
run a query that

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:19.370
joins the data
between that key set

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:22.158
table and the encrypted data
that you have in BigQuery.

00:22:22.158 --> 00:22:24.200
And that gives you a way
of performing decryption

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:27.230
in BigQuery without ever having
to keep the key sets themselves

00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:29.093
stored in BigQuery metadata.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.620
So finally, I've got a couple
of cool things to announce.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:35.870
These are alpha announcements.

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:39.170
So, as you may be
familiar, alpha features

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.160
are not available
for anyone to use.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:44.220
We have specific
programs around these.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:45.870
So if you want access
to one of these,

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:47.620
please talk to whoever
your Google contact

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.350
is, whether it's a salesperson
or a customer engineer, et

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:50.850
cetera.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:52.933
And they can help set you
up with access to these.

00:22:52.933 --> 00:22:55.520
This is sort of an early preview
of some upcoming features

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.358
that we think that
you'll really like.

00:22:57.358 --> 00:22:59.150
The first of these that
I'd like to mention

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:00.590
is our scripting alpha.

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.180
So scripting gives
you the ability

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:05.210
to send multiple queries
in one request to BigQuery.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.250
It also lets you use variables.

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:11.060
And then, furthermore, you
can use conditions and loops

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:13.970
to actually manage the flow
of execution of the script

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:15.350
that you send to BigQuery.

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:17.000
So this is a really
powerful feature.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:18.500
Historically, we've
always supported

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.780
running a single query
at a time in BigQuery.

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:23.433
So this lets you build
a lot more functionality

00:23:23.433 --> 00:23:25.350
into BigQuery directly,
where you just hand us

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.892
the script of all the things
you want to accomplish and we go

00:23:27.892 --> 00:23:29.092
and run it for you.

00:23:29.092 --> 00:23:30.800
And again, if you want
to access to this,

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:32.550
please talk to whoever
your Google contact

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:36.080
is about enrolling.

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:39.890
Secondly, we're announcing
the alpha persistent UDFs.

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:43.130
So there was just a comment
on our public issue tracker

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:43.672
this morning.

00:23:43.672 --> 00:23:45.547
It's been two and a half
years since somebody

00:23:45.547 --> 00:23:46.740
requested this feature.

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.092
Why haven't you done this yet?

00:23:48.092 --> 00:23:48.800
Well, here it is.

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:50.000
So we have this alpha now.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.262
You can come and try
it if you want to.

00:23:52.262 --> 00:23:54.720
It's been a long time in the
making, but it's finally here.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.660
So if you've ever wanted
to reuse functions

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:58.520
across different queries,
or maybe somebody

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:00.320
has created a bunch of functions
and wants to share them

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.900
with you, you can now create
them and then share them

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:04.367
with anybody that you want to.

00:24:04.367 --> 00:24:06.200
So this gives you a way
of reusing functions

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:08.960
instead of having to put them
all at the top of your queries,

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:09.460
now.

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:14.115
And so with that, that's
the end of my section.

00:24:14.115 --> 00:24:16.240
Please welcome Subra to
the stage, from Home Depot.

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:23.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:25.640
SUBRA DHURJATI: Thanks, Elliot.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:29.090
Most of the features mentioned
are really game-changing here.

00:24:29.090 --> 00:24:29.880
Good morning, all.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:33.260
I'm super excited to
be here before you.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:37.040
My aim is to talk about
some of our experiences

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.910
in implementing an enterprise
data warehouse on the Google

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:40.410
platform.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.410
Have an agenda here.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:47.950
Talk a little bit
about The Home Depot--

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:50.080
cool, interesting facts.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.820
Switch to enterprise
data warehouse,

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:55.590
which is my group here.

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:58.580
And then we touch upon
the facts and dimensions

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.250
before I'm going to speak
about the challenges.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.980
I'm very sure everyone
has shopped at Home Depot.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:09.490
We are big.

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:12.340
We are the number
one home improvement

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:14.110
retailer in the world.

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:18.730
We have 153
distribution centers.

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:22.870
And we have like
$100 billion sales.

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:29.360
My group, specifically-- the
enterprise data warehouse.

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:33.690
We started the journey to
implement our enterprise data

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:38.330
warehouse on the BigQuery
sometime around 2016.

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:41.960
Today, we have close to
16 petabytes of data.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:45.050
As we speak, we are
still growing, actually.

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:50.780
We have 40,000 users across
the stores, corporate centers,

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:51.800
distribution centers.

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:55.550
And also, we do a lot of
mobile reporting and analytics

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:57.065
on the mobile devices.

00:25:59.630 --> 00:26:02.240
I'm very sure everybody
knows your staff schema.

00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:06.750
The intent is to show what it
means to us, as Home Depot.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.910
With all the 5,000
tables that I mention,

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:12.050
if I draw this diagram, I'm
very sure it will cover the most

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:14.190
part of the wall behind me.

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:17.970
But as a simplicity here,
what I'm trying to show is,

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:19.460
we have sales.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:22.880
We have orders and supply
chain, customer orders,

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:26.180
the classic retail
dimensions like the product,

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:32.530
the vendor, the stores, and
the customer, obviously.

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:35.980
Touch upon a minute to
talk about what facts are.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.580
The facts means
metrics, or the measures

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:41.370
that are some [INAUDIBLE]
tools talk about.

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:43.630
Transactional in
nature, usually.

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:44.910
They could be streamed.

00:26:44.910 --> 00:26:46.810
They could be in batch.

00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:50.580
And other important thing
is, they can be additive.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:54.590
Example-- sales can be
rolled across weeks or time.

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:57.630
Or, a classic thing is,
inventory is a snapshot.

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:00.220
You cannot roll it over time.

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:03.040
Next slide.

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:04.270
Here, master data.

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:05.970
Comparatively, they
are smaller in size.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:08.170
They have attributes.

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:10.560
Example is a screw size.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:12.970
A nail or a screw
in Home Depot could

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:17.630
be 3/8 size each or 1/14 size.

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:19.040
There's changing.

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:21.170
They change over time.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.650
And they are hierarchical.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:26.080
Example-- organization
hierarchy could

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:29.250
have a location
moving to the district

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:32.500
all the way up to the division.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:36.090
Also, here, the primary
purpose of all the dimensions

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.970
here is to slice
and dice your facts

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:41.430
and then to drill up and down.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.400
The roll up functions,
as we call them.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.115
Challenges [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:46.115 --> 00:27:49.140
I know as we are implementing
our enterprise data whereas--

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:51.100
any platform for
that matter, there'll

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:54.320
be tons of challenges, numerous.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:57.690
And I could spend pretty
much the whole day

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.350
talking about these.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.850
But because of
interest of time, I'll

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.190
narrow the scope to
just the dimensions,

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:09.630
and also touch upon
a few of these.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:14.040
Whenever we are implementing
a warehouse, especially

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.890
in the data
integration space, we

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:19.770
want to find out, hey, what
has changed in the source?

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:21.720
And do a delta processing.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.160
That's one.

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:26.760
And there's a big deal in pretty
much all the business, I guess,

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:29.310
where from a
business perspective,

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:32.640
we want to talk about as-is
was as as-was reporting.

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.610
I'll explain what that
is in the next slides.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:40.220
And the other thing I want to
touch upon is the versioning.

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:42.300
How do we store history?

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:45.300
By the way, most of the
points are interlinked.

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.440
One key thing, again,
I want to mention

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:50.130
here is about surrogate keys.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:53.730
One of the lessons learned with
the advent of data scientists

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.970
and analysts who can
write their own queries--

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:01.480
what we have found is, it's
better to avoid surrogate keys.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:08.070
They're very 1999-ish
to the early 2000.

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:10.920
It confuses them, because you
have to now use surrogate keys

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:12.180
to do all the joins.

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:14.250
It's good for
operational reporting,

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:16.530
but not for deep analytics.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:20.220
That's the lesson we learned.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:21.990
Change data capture.

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:24.720
I'm very sure we have a lot of--

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:26.490
every company has
a lot of sources

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:29.010
from which we extract the data.

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:33.030
Some of the sources, especially
with respect to legacy,

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:37.510
doesn't have a method to
know what has changed.

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:41.890
There's no timestamp column
to know what has changed.

00:29:41.890 --> 00:29:43.300
That's one scenario.

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:47.550
And there are other cases where
the source has a last update

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:50.820
timestamp which you
can use to extract

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.700
for the delta processing.

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:56.480
The third-- there are
sophisticated systems where,

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:59.280
for example, with database
replication, they can tell,

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:02.080
hey, these are all the
changes that you have.

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:07.250
And that makes life easier
in data integration space.

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:10.130
As-is as well as
as-was reporting.

00:30:10.130 --> 00:30:11.850
It's a big deal,
actually, if you

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:14.380
look from a business
perspective,

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:17.020
especially in Home Depot.

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:20.070
As I mentioned in
the earlier slides,

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:21.850
the dimensions are hierarchical.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:22.900
They change over time.

00:30:22.900 --> 00:30:24.150
They could be still changing.

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:27.250
But they change over time.

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:30.870
So most of the businesses-- they
want to see all the metrics,

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:34.170
analyze all the metrics to
the current point in time,

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:36.090
the most recent hierarchy.

00:30:36.090 --> 00:30:40.740
Example-- as you move to a
different department and they

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:45.090
want to see all the sales
rolled up to that department,

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:46.200
what it means--

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.660
I'm very sure a lot
of companies do build

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:52.390
aggregates for performance.

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:55.440
To [INAUDIBLE] single
version of the truth,

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:59.190
all the aggregates
need to be restated.

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:02.680
We call that internally,
in Home Depot, as recast.

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:06.240
So every day, we recast
all of our metrics

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:10.830
to the most recent hierarchy.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:15.090
But there are business
analysts and data scientists,

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:18.480
especially in the legal
departments and the marketing,

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:23.695
who want to see all the metrics
to a particular point in time.

00:31:23.695 --> 00:31:25.355
You have to go
back like, example,

00:31:25.355 --> 00:31:30.720
give me my sales as of
the hierarchy in 2007,

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:36.780
or give me the address of
the customer as of 2010.

00:31:36.780 --> 00:31:40.260
To do that, we have to
enable as-was reporting.

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:42.810
To do all of these, you
need to keep the history.

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:45.480
That's the point here, I guess.

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:47.350
Next slide.

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:48.690
This slide is multifold.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:49.710
I want to talk--

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:53.340
I know I put as one
of the methodologies

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.950
for versioning to
implement our history,

00:31:55.950 --> 00:32:01.170
but I'm also going to talk
about our Home Depot methodology

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:04.670
here, of how we do data
integration with the ELT

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:06.670
approach.

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:08.940
And the third point I
want to also make here

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:14.190
is, adaptable to use case
where the source does not

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.260
tell me what has changed.

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:21.480
So I'm going to go through that
use case, too, in this slide.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:26.370
Going from left to right,
we have the source table.

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:27.750
It could be mainframe.

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:30.660
It doesn't have a
last update timestamp.

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:34.660
So our integration methodology
in the data injection

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:38.150
is to extract that
into a raw data set.

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:40.510
When I say raw, it
means so similar.

00:32:40.510 --> 00:32:44.470
It's pretty much close
to what source is.

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:46.430
And then we have
to scrub the data.

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:47.310
So we scrub it.

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:51.670
We're using [INAUDIBLE]
bunch of BigQuery functions

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:54.400
that we can use
to scrub the data

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:57.350
and then land it into
a staging database.

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:00.820
Again, this is an ELTV
loading to BigQuery.

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:06.040
And then do all our
massaging of data there.

00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:09.040
And then at that point,
now the data is pretty much

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:11.530
what we need, I guess.

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:14.620
But as I said, the source
didn't tell me what has changed.

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:16.370
It has all the rules.

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:19.690
So now, I'm going to go
to my yesterday's copy,

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:23.470
or the previous run's copy of
my target [INAUDIBLE] full outer

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:27.780
join figure out
what has changed.

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.400
If it is in the source
but not in target,

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:31.830
that means there are new rules.

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:34.730
If it is in target
but not in the source,

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:37.210
that means something
else got deleted.

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:41.180
If it is in both and the
columns have some different,

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.630
that means they're updated.

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:46.240
We take up all these
changes, stack them

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:54.390
up in a table which I'm calling
it as Change Capture Table.

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:59.530
And if you see, that's
in the data data set.

00:33:59.530 --> 00:34:02.040
So we have a raw,
which is so similar.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:05.140
We have a stage where it
is temporarily massaged.

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:09.489
And then the final is
all in the data data set.

00:34:09.489 --> 00:34:13.489
The other thing is, now we have
to do the as-is was is as-was.

00:34:13.489 --> 00:34:16.719
So to do the as-is, we use
BigQuery functions like

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:20.340
rollover right at the analytical
functions to get the most

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:24.130
recent row and put it in
the target, which is our--

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:27.060
we expose to business.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:29.730
I didn't represent
the as-was case,

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:33.210
but you could always use
the analytical functions

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:35.969
like lead, lag, all
those functions,

00:34:35.969 --> 00:34:39.300
and get to effective
begin and end dates and do

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:41.610
the as-was reporting.

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:46.080
One thing to point out here
is, from our legacy warehouse

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:51.250
to which we moved to BigQuery,
we do a one-time conversion.

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:53.575
And I have to put that
data into the target.

00:34:56.449 --> 00:34:57.320
The same thing, now.

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:00.900
What I did is, I explained
using the data here.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:05.600
And one thing I want to point
out is, there's an active flag.

00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:07.640
So if a record has
got deleted, we

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:09.645
make the active flag as false.

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:13.765
Moving on.

00:35:13.765 --> 00:35:16.480
Pros of this approach--
it's very easy to implement

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:18.070
and it's self-healing.

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:20.810
You basically stack
up all the changes.

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:24.840
Let's say if some
transaction comes late,

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:28.617
it still goes in and
self-heals because there's

00:35:28.617 --> 00:35:29.700
nothing much we are doing.

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:32.906
We are stacking up and
pulling the latest.

00:35:32.906 --> 00:35:35.360
It works for even stream data.

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:40.320
Though my example said that
I am doing a full extract,

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:46.901
this approach works beautifully
well for the streaming.

00:35:46.901 --> 00:35:48.440
Cons.

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:51.110
One of the cons I
heard from business,

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:54.980
if they are not
that SQL-savvy, is,

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:59.450
you have to use some analytical
functions to get the as-was.

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:03.830
But to mitigate it, we could
always precreate the tables

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:06.220
and export them.

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:10.550
Then, if the tables
are huge, I know

00:36:10.550 --> 00:36:12.860
sometimes the customer
dimension could

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:17.120
and similar dimensions could
be multi-million to billion

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:17.910
records.

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:21.740
There might be some
performance issues.

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:25.730
If you're joining to, really,
a multi-billion fact table,

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:28.040
that's one thing
to keep in mind.

00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:32.430
We have the next approach.

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:34.710
Again, this slide
is also multifold.

00:36:34.710 --> 00:36:38.400
Here, I've used
streaming use case.

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:40.610
But it could work
even for a patch.

00:36:40.610 --> 00:36:43.860
Just for representation,
I put the streaming.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.710
Moving from left to
right, in this case,

00:36:46.710 --> 00:36:48.240
the source is sophisticated.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:50.220
And by the way, these
are live examples

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:52.990
that we are running
in our warehouse.

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:54.660
So it is sophisticated.

00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:57.960
They use a data
replication technology

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:01.170
to identify, give me all the
changes, including, they say,

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:02.060
these are inserts.

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:02.880
These are updates.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:05.410
And these are deletes.

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:10.170
And we use a transport process
to put it onto the Pub/Sub.

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:15.720
From this point, we want EV
[INAUDIBLE] this data now.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:18.600
So again, sticking
back to our Home Depot

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:22.100
methodology and [INAUDIBLE]
methodology here,

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:23.880
is to put it in raw.

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:28.410
We use the Dataflow to put it
into our raw database with all

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:30.270
these changes.

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:36.000
From that point, we do a
bunch of transformations

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:38.490
because we are following
the ELT approach, here.

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:40.800
We could do something
that pops up.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:45.000
But for these, we are
especially using ELT--

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:47.970
reason being, our
technical staff

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:50.510
are really familiar
with the SQL.

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:52.860
Our existing, earlier
legacy warehouse

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:56.800
had a bunch of SQLs
to do the ELTs,

00:37:56.800 --> 00:38:00.970
so it was seamless for
faster development.

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:03.090
We put it into the staging.

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:09.480
And then we use the BigQuery
merge DMO statements,

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.660
which I believe was
launched around a year ago.

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:13.560
I think so.

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:15.940
So once this was done,
most of our tables

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:18.830
now use this merge
statements with the effective

00:38:18.830 --> 00:38:21.690
begin and end dates
already in the table.

00:38:24.620 --> 00:38:25.640
Next slide.

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:29.831
The same thing I explained as
example with the data here.

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:34.340
Pros and cons.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:38.170
Compared to the other one, the
pro is, the effective begin

00:38:38.170 --> 00:38:40.805
and end date is
already in the table.

00:38:40.805 --> 00:38:48.110
And you could do the as-was
and as-is type of reportings

00:38:48.110 --> 00:38:51.710
easily using the effective
begin and end dates.

00:38:56.210 --> 00:39:00.190
What we have found is, this
approach works well for tables

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:00.690
with--

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:04.050
I think we have a couple of
dimensions with a billion

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:07.830
records' worth with 80 columns,
and it still works well.

00:39:10.500 --> 00:39:13.380
The cons-- it's
slightly complex.

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:16.280
If we stay in stacking,
we just stack it up

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:18.060
and pull the latest,
whereas here, you

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:23.590
have to take it off some
level of programming here.

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.540
And the second one-- when
we are doing streaming,

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:30.920
it adds a different level of
complexity here, the reason

00:39:30.920 --> 00:39:33.230
being, you have to
now use effective

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:35.840
begin and end timestamps
rather than dates.

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:39.170
It adds a slight
layer of complexity.

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:43.870
What we learned.

00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:46.810
So obviously, there
are a lot of challenges

00:39:46.810 --> 00:39:53.020
with when you're doing a data
integration in a big enterprise

00:39:53.020 --> 00:39:56.350
data warehouse space.

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:59.260
We are using the ELT
methodology because BigQuery

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:01.050
has all the rich functions.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:05.150
And still, I haven't found
something that is not available

00:40:05.150 --> 00:40:08.100
in BigQuery that we cannot
use to cleanse our data,

00:40:08.100 --> 00:40:09.520
pretty much.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:12.910
We use this approach to
do our data integration.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:15.430
And it so far is working well.

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:19.270
And we migrated,
integrated close

00:40:19.270 --> 00:40:22.510
to 5,000 tables in
less than a year.

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:26.080
Hey.

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:28.510
Spring is finally here.

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:31.040
It's time for yard
work and mulch and--

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:34.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:37.960
I would encourage you, stop by
your local Home Depot store.

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:38.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:43.600
[INAUDIBLE] one of my coworker
there will help you all.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:44.180
Thank you.

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:45.790
Thank you all.

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:48.840
